 "captions": [
  {
   "text": "The internet ran out of addresses.",
   "hide": true
  },
  {
   "text": "Not in theory."
  },
  {
   "text": "On the third of February 2011, the last free blocks were handed out, at a ceremony in Miami.",
   "say": "On the third of February, twenty eleven, the last free blocks were handed out, at a ceremony in Miami."
  },
  {
   "text": "And yet you're watching this."
  },
  {
   "text": "Your phone is online. So is your TV, your laptop, maybe even your fridge."
  },
  {
   "text": "So how does an internet with !no addresses left! keep growing?"
  },
  {
   "text": "The answer involves a guess from 1977, a trick hiding in your router,",
   "say": "The answer involves a guess from nineteen seventy-seven, a trick hiding in your router,"
  },
  {
   "text": "a market where addresses sell for twenty dollars each, and a fix that took thirty years."
  },
  {
   "text": "Every device on the internet needs an address, like a phone number, so data knows where to go."
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1977, Vint Cerf, one of the engineers who designed the internet's core protocols,",
   "say": "In nineteen seventy-seven, Vint Cerf, one of the engineers who designed the internet's core protocols,"
  },
  {
   "text": "had to decide how long those addresses would be."
  },
  {
   "text": "Some colleagues wanted *128 bits.* Others called that crazy. Cerf picked !32.!",
   "say": "Some colleagues wanted a hundred and twenty-eight bits. Others called that crazy. Cerf picked thirty-two."
  }
 ],
